---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, TRADE_TERMS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'Battery Shipping, Packaging & Warranty | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'How lead-acid batteries are packaged, shipped and warranted — UN 2794/2800 Class 8 classification, the export document set, and how to read a warranty scope.';
const articleTitle = 'Battery shipping, packaging and warranty';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Are lead-acid batteries dangerous goods?', a: 'Yes — Class 8 corrosive. Flooded starting batteries ship as UN 2794 (filled with acid) and non-spillable designs as UN 2800, but unlike lithium they need no UN 38.3 test report.' },
  { q: 'Why does battery MOQ follow the container?', a: 'Because batteries are heavy — a 20ft container is capped at about 28,000 kg of payload, and the load hits that weight ceiling before it fills the 33 m³ of volume.' },
  { q: 'What documents does a battery export need?', a: '5 at minimum — commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin and a dangerous-goods declaration — plus test reports and MSDS where the destination requires them.' },
  { q: 'What does a 12-month warranty actually cover?', a: 'Only what the written scope says — duration, exclusions and claim handling. A "12-month warranty" with broad exclusions is not the same as one with clear, narrow ones.' },
  { q: 'How should batteries be packed for sea freight?', a: 'Upright, terminals protected from shorting, on export-grade pallets kept to about 1.5 m height and 1.5 tonnes per pallet, so the load survives handling and customs inspection.' }
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Logistics" dateModified="2026-09-10" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · Logistics</span>
    <h1>Battery shipping, packaging and warranty</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Sources: IMO IMDG Code, US DOT PHMSA, export logistics practice</p>
    <p class="lead">A battery order is not complete when it leaves the line — it is complete when it arrives undamaged, with the right documents, and with a warranty you understand. This page covers the three parts that decide whether a shipment actually delivers its value: classification, packaging, and warranty scope.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Lead-acid batteries are Class 8 (corrosive) dangerous goods — UN 2794 filled with acid, or UN 2800 non-spillable — but, unlike lithium, they do not require a UN 38.3 report. Packaging must keep terminals from shorting and the case from impact, the export set needs at least five documents, and a warranty is only as good as its written duration, exclusions and claim handling.
    </div>

    <h2>Dangerous-goods classification</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid batteries are not general cargo. The electrolyte is sulfuric acid, so the product is Class 8 corrosive under the [IMO IMDG Code](https://www.imo.org/), which governs sea freight, with the UN numbers themselves set by the [UN Model Regulations](https://unece.org/transport/dangerous-goods). Flooded batteries ship as <strong>UN 2794 — Batteries, wet, filled with acid</strong>; sealed, non-spillable designs that pass vibration and pressure-differential tests ship as <strong>UN 2800 — Batteries, wet, non-spillable</strong>. The full logic — including the UN 2800 test requirements and the comparison with lithium's UN 3480/UN 3481 — is in the <a href="/knowledge/lead-acid-battery-dangerous-goods/">dangerous-goods classification</a> page.</p>

    <h2>Packaging and container loading</h2>
    <p>Batteries are dense, so the loading math works backwards: a standard 20ft container offers about 33 m³ of volume but only about 28,000 kg of payload, and a battery load hits the weight ceiling before the volume. That is why MOQ follows the container (typically {TRADE_TERMS.moq}). Packaging has three jobs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Terminal protection</strong> — prevent short circuits between adjacent batteries.</li>
      <li><strong>Impact protection</strong> — keep the case intact through handling and container movement.</li>
      <li><strong>Upright orientation</strong> — keep flooded batteries upright so acid stays in the cells.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pallets are usually kept to about 1.5 m height and 1.5 tonnes for safe stacking, and mixed-model loading spreads unit cost across a full container. The <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-moq-lead-time/">MOQ and lead time</a> page explains the weight logic in detail.</p>

    <h2>Export documentation</h2>
    <p>A clean export set avoids delays at customs. The baseline five documents:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Commercial invoice and packing list</li>
      <li>Bill of lading</li>
      <li>Certificate of origin</li>
      <li>Dangerous-goods declaration (the one amateurs miss)</li>
      <li>Test reports (CCA / capacity) and MSDS where required</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Shipping terms follow {TRADE_TERMS.incoterms}, with export from {MANUFACTURER.exportPorts}. Each document and what it proves is in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-export-documentation/">export documentation</a> page.</p>

    <h2>Warranty: read the scope, not just the number</h2>
    <p>Warranty is {TRADE_TERMS.warranty}. But a warranty is only as good as its written scope. Confirm three things before relying on it:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Duration</strong> — from bill of lading date, not from your customer's purchase.</li>
      <li><strong>Exclusions</strong> — what is <em>not</em> covered: deep discharge, misuse, physical damage, poor charging.</li>
      <li><strong>Handling</strong> — how a claim is filed, inspected and settled, and whether settlement is full replacement or pro-rata.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">A &ldquo;12-month warranty&rdquo; with broad exclusions is not the same as a 12-month warranty with clear, narrow ones — and a pro-rata settlement is worth less than a full replacement. Get the terms in writing.</p>
    <p>The claim-side detail — how a failure is judged, how credit is calculated and how long it takes — is in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-warranty-claim-process/">warranty claim process</a> page.</p>

    <h2>Storage: the warranty you lose silently</h2>
    <p>Packaging and warranty both assume the battery ships soon after production. A battery that sits in a warehouse is still discharging — lead-acid self-discharges at roughly 3–5% per month, and below about 50% charge sulfation begins to harden on the plates. Store cool and fully charged, and top up every 6 months. The <a href="/knowledge/battery-storage-shelf-life/">storage and shelf life</a> page turns this into a warehouse procedure.</p>

    <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>How are batteries packed and loaded — is the container weight-limited or volume-limited?</li>
      <li>Which export documents are provided as standard, and who signs the dangerous-goods declaration?</li>
      <li>What are the exact warranty exclusions and claim handling — full replacement or pro-rata?</li>
      <li>What Incoterm applies, and where does risk transfer?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where this fits</h2>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP} This page sits inside the logistics and warranty cluster:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/battery-shipping-guide/">Battery shipping, compliance and warranty: the complete guide</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/lead-acid-battery-dangerous-goods/">Lead-acid battery dangerous-goods classification</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/battery-export-documentation/">Battery export documentation</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/battery-warranty-claim-process/">Battery warranty claim process</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/battery-storage-shelf-life/">Battery storage and shelf life</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>See how lead time and MOQ work in the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-moq-lead-time/">MOQ and lead time guide</a>, and how to lock terms into an <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">RFQ</a>.</p>
    <p><a href="/contact/">Battery shipping and warranty terms &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <ArticleFaq faqs={faqs} />

  <CTASection title="Confirm shipping and warranty terms" />
</ArticleLayout>
